\id GEN SAGE bounded evidence packet
\c 9
\v 18 The sons of Noah who came out of the ark were Shem, Ham and Japheth. (Ham was the father of Canaan.)
\v 19 These were the three sons of Noah, and from them came the people who were scattered over the whole earth.
\v 20 Noah, a man of the soil, proceeded\f + \fr 9:20 \ft Or \fq soil, \fqa was the first\f* to plant a vineyard.
\v 21 When he drank some of its wine, he became drunk and lay uncovered inside his tent.
\v 22 Ham, the father of Canaan, saw his father naked and told his two brothers outside.
\v 23 But Shem and Japheth took a garment and laid it across their shoulders; then they walked in backward and covered their fatherʼs naked body. Their faces were turned the other way so that they would not see their father naked.
\v 24 When Noah awoke from his wine and found out what his youngest son had done to him,
\v 25 he said,
\q1 “Cursed be Canaan!
\q2 The lowest of slaves
\q2 will he be to his brothers.”
\v 26 He also said,
\q1 “Praise be to the \nd Lord\nd*, the God of Shem!
\q2 May Canaan be the slave of Shem.
\v 27 May God extend Japhethʼs\f + \fr 9:27 \fq Japheth \ft sounds like the Hebrew for \fq extend.\f* territory;
\q2 may Japheth live in the tents of Shem,
\q2 and may Canaan be the slave of Japheth.”
\v 28 After the flood Noah lived 350 years.
\v 29 Noah lived a total of 950 years, and then he died.
\c 10
\v 1 This is the account of Shem, Ham and Japheth, Noahʼs sons, who themselves had sons after the flood.
\v 2 The sons\f + \fr 10:2 \fq Sons \ft may mean \fqa descendants \ft or \fqa successors \ft or \fqa nations\ft ; also in \+xt verses 3|GEN 10:3\+xt*, \+xt 4|GEN 10:4\+xt*, \+xt 6|GEN 10:6\+xt*, \+xt 7|GEN 10:7\+xt*, \+xt 20‑23|GEN 10:20-23\+xt*, \+xt 29|GEN 10:29\+xt* and \+xt 31|GEN 10:31\+xt*.\f* of Japheth:
\li2 Gomer, Magog, Madai, Javan, Tubal, Meshek and Tiras.
\v 3 The sons of Gomer:
\li2 Ashkenaz, Riphath and Togarmah.
\v 4 The sons of Javan:
\li2 Elishah, Tarshish, the Kittites and the Rodanites.\f + \fr 10:4 \ft Some manuscripts of the Masoretic Text and Samaritan Pentateuch (see also Septuagint and \+xt 1 Chron. 1:7\+xt*); most manuscripts of the Masoretic Text \fqa Dodanites\f*
\v 5 (From these the maritime peoples spread out into their territories by their clans within their nations, each with its own language.)
\v 6 The sons of Ham:
\li2 Cush, Egypt, Put and Canaan.
\v 7 The sons of Cush:
\li2 Seba, Havilah, Sabtah, Raamah and Sabteka.
\li1 The sons of Raamah:
\li2 Sheba and Dedan.
\v 8 Cush was the father\f + \fr 10:8 \fq Father \ft may mean \fqa ancestor \ft or \fqa predecessor \ft or \fqa founder; \ft also in \+xt verses 13|GEN 10:13\+xt*, \+xt 15|GEN 10:15\+xt*, \+xt 24|GEN 10:24\+xt* and \+xt 26|GEN 10:26\+xt*.\f* of Nimrod, who became a mighty warrior on the earth.
\v 9 He was a mighty hunter before the \nd Lord\nd*; that is why it is said, “Like Nimrod, a mighty hunter before the \nd Lord\nd*.”
\v 10 The first centers of his kingdom were Babylon, Uruk, Akkad and Kalneh, in\f + \fr 10:10 \ft Or \fq Uruk \fqa and Akkad—all of them in\f* Shinar.\f + \fr 10:10 \ft That is, Babylonia\f*
\v 11 From that land he went to Assyria, where he built Nineveh, Rehoboth Ir,\f + \fr 10:11 \ft Or \fq Nineveh \fqa with its city squares\f* Calah
\v 12 and Resen, which is between Nineveh and Calah—which is the great city.
\v 13 Egypt was the father of
\li2 the Ludites, Anamites, Lehabites, Naphtuhites,
\v 14 Pathrusites, Kasluhites (from whom the Philistines came) and Caphtorites.
\v 15 Canaan was the father of
\li2 Sidon his firstborn,\f + \fr 10:15 \ft Or \fq of \fqa the Sidonians, the foremost\f* and of the Hittites,
\v 16 Jebusites, Amorites, Girgashites,
\v 17 Hivites, Arkites, Sinites,
\v 18 Arvadites, Zemarites and Hamathites.
\li2 (Later the Canaanite clans scattered
\v 19 and the borders of Canaan reached from Sidon toward Gerar as far as Gaza, and then toward Sodom, Gomorrah, Admah and Zeboyim, as far as Lasha.)
\v 20 These are the sons of Ham by their clans and languages, in their territories and nations.
\v 21 Sons were also born to Shem, whose older brother was\f + \fr 10:21 \ft Or \fq Shem, \fqa the older brother of\f* Japheth; Shem was the ancestor of all the sons of Eber.
\v 22 The sons of Shem:
\li2 Elam, Ashur, Arphaxad, Lud and Aram.
\v 23 The sons of Aram:
\li2 Uz, Hul, Gether and Meshek.\f + \fr 10:23 \ft See Septuagint and \+xt 1 Chron. 1:17\+xt*; Hebrew \fqa Mash.\f*
\v 24 Arphaxad was the father of\f + \fr 10:24 \ft Hebrew; Septuagint \fq father \fqa of Cainan, and Cainan was the father of\f* Shelah,
\li2 and Shelah the father of Eber.
\v 25 Two sons were born to Eber:
\li2 One was named Peleg,\f + \fr 10:25 \fq Peleg \ft means \fqa division.\f* because in his time the earth was divided; his brother was named Joktan.
\v 26 Joktan was the father of
\li2 Almodad, Sheleph, Hazarmaveth, Jerah,
\v 27 Hadoram, Uzal, Diklah,
\v 28 Obal, Abimael, Sheba,
\v 29 Ophir, Havilah and Jobab. All these were sons of Joktan.
\v 30 (The region where they lived stretched from Mesha toward Sephar, in the eastern hill country.)
\v 31 These are the sons of Shem by their clans and languages, in their territories and nations.
\v 32 These are the clans of Noahʼs sons, according to their lines of descent, within their nations. From these the nations spread out over the earth after the flood.
\c 11
\v 1 Now the whole world had one language and a common speech.
\v 2 As people moved eastward,\f + \fr 11:2 \ft Or \fqa from the east\ft ; or \fqa in the east\f* they found a plain in Shinar\f + \cat dup\cat*\fr 11:2 \ft That is, Babylonia\f* and settled there.
\v 3 They said to each other, “Come, letʼs make bricks and bake them thoroughly.” They used brick instead of stone, and tar for mortar.
\v 4 Then they said, “Come, let us build ourselves a city, with a tower that reaches to the heavens, so that we may make a name for ourselves; otherwise we will be scattered over the face of the whole earth.”
\v 5 But the \nd Lord\nd* came down to see the city and the tower the people were building.
\v 6 The \nd Lord\nd* said, “If as one people speaking the same language they have begun to do this, then nothing they plan to do will be impossible for them.
\v 7 Come, let us go down and confuse their language so they will not understand each other.”
\v 8 So the \nd Lord\nd* scattered them from there over all the earth, and they stopped building the city.
\v 9 That is why it was called Babel\f + \fr 11:9 \ft That is, Babylon; \fq Babel \ft sounds like the Hebrew for \fq confused.\f*—because there the \nd Lord\nd* confused the language of the whole world. From there the \nd Lord\nd* scattered them over the face of the whole earth.
\v 10 This is the account of Shemʼs family line.
\li1 Two years after the flood, when Shem was 100 years old, he became the father\f + \fr 11:10 \fq Father \ft may mean \fqa ancestor\ft ; also in \+xt verses 11‑25|GEN 11:11-25\+xt*.\f* of Arphaxad.
\v 11 And after he became the father of Arphaxad, Shem lived 500 years and had other sons and daughters.
\v 12 When Arphaxad had lived 35 years, he became the father of Shelah.
\v 13 And after he became the father of Shelah, Arphaxad lived 403 years and had other sons and daughters.\f + \fr 11:12,13 \ft Hebrew; Septuagint (see also \+xt Luke 3:35,36\+xt* and note at \+xt Gen. 10:24\+xt*) \fq 35 years, he became the father of \fqa Cainan. \+fv 13\+fv*And after he became the father of Cainan, Arphaxad lived 430 years and had other sons and daughters, and then he died. When Cainan had lived 130 years, he became the father of Shelah. And after he became the father of Shelah, Cainan lived 330 years and had other sons and daughters\f*
\v 14 When Shelah had lived 30 years, he became the father of Eber.
\v 15 And after he became the father of Eber, Shelah lived 403 years and had other sons and daughters.
\v 16 When Eber had lived 34 years, he became the father of Peleg.
\v 17 And after he became the father of Peleg, Eber lived 430 years and had other sons and daughters.
\v 18 When Peleg had lived 30 years, he became the father of Reu.
\v 19 And after he became the father of Reu, Peleg lived 209 years and had other sons and daughters.
\v 20 When Reu had lived 32 years, he became the father of Serug.
\v 21 And after he became the father of Serug, Reu lived 207 years and had other sons and daughters.
\v 22 When Serug had lived 30 years, he became the father of Nahor.
\v 23 And after he became the father of Nahor, Serug lived 200 years and had other sons and daughters.
\v 24 When Nahor had lived 29 years, he became the father of Terah.
\v 25 And after he became the father of Terah, Nahor lived 119 years and had other sons and daughters.
\v 26 After Terah had lived 70 years, he became the father of Abram, Nahor and Haran.
\v 27 This is the account of Terahʼs family line.
\p Terah became the father of Abram, Nahor and Haran. And Haran became the father of Lot.
\v 28 While his father Terah was still alive, Haran died in Ur of the Chaldeans, in the land of his birth.
\v 29 Abram and Nahor both married. The name of Abramʼs wife was Sarai, and the name of Nahorʼs wife was Milkah; she was the daughter of Haran, the father of both Milkah and Iskah.
\v 30 Now Sarai was childless because she was not able to conceive.
\v 31 Terah took his son Abram, his grandson Lot son of Haran, and his daughter-in-law Sarai, the wife of his son Abram, and together they set out from Ur of the Chaldeans to go to Canaan. But when they came to Harran, they settled there.
\v 32 Terah lived 205 years, and he died in Harran.
